+Let $A \sub \VF^n$ be a definable set and $f : A \fun \mdl P(\VF)$ a definable finitary function as in Lemma~\ref{at:rv:split}. Then there are a definable finite partition $(A_i)_i$ of $A$ and, for each $i$ and all open polydiscs $\ga \sub A_i$, finitely many $(\code\ga,t_{\ga j})$-definable functions $f_{\ga j} : \ga \fun t^\sharp_{\ga j}$, where $t_{\ga j} \in \RV_\infty$, such that  $f \rest \ga = \bigcup_j f_{\ga j}$  and each $f_{\ga j}(\ga)$ is either a point or an open disc.
 \end{cor}
 \begin{proof}
-For $a \in A$, let $D_a \sub A$ be the type-definable subset containing $a$. By Lemma~\ref{atom:self}, every open polydisc $\ga \sub D_a$ is $\code \ga$-atomic and hence, by Lemma~\ref{atomic:open:ball:open}, the assertion holds for $\ga$. Then, by compactness, the assertion must hold in a definable subset $A_a \sub A$ that contains $a$; by compactness again, it holds in finitely many definable subsets $A_1, \ldots, A_m$ of $A$ with $\bigcup_i A_i = A$. Then the partition of $A$ generated by $A_1, \ldots, A_m$ is as desired.
+By compactness, there is a uniform finite bound $N$ on the cardinalities of the fibers $f(a)$. For an open-polydisc code $d$, write $\ga_d$ for its polydisc and let $P(d)$ say that $\rv\circ(f\rest\ga_d)$ is constant and that the image of each of its single-valued $\rv$-tagged branches is a point or an open disc. This is a first-order condition: use the finite disjunction over the possible numbers $0\leq k\leq N$ of distinct tags, and express a point or an open disc by its usual uniform codes. Injectivity of $\rv$ on each fiber gives uniqueness of each tagged branch.
+
+For $a\in A$, let $D_a\sub A$ be the complete type-definable set containing $a$. By Lemma~\ref{atom:self}, every open polydisc $\ga_d\sub D_a$ is $\code\ga_d$-atomic. Lemma~\ref{atomic:open:multidisc}, over the ground base together with this disc parameter, therefore gives $P(d)$. If no finite part of the type of $a$ had this property for every polydisc contained in it, compactness would produce an open-polydisc code $d$ with $\ga_d\sub D_a$ and $\neg P(d)$, a contradiction. Thus there is a ground-definable set $A_a\sub A$ containing $a$ such that $P(d)$ holds whenever $\ga_d\sub A_a$. A second application of compactness gives finitely many such sets covering $A$; take the partition they generate.
+
+For a polydisc $\ga$ in a partition piece, choose its finitely many distinct tags $t_{\ga j}\in\RV_\infty$. The full graph of its $j$th branch is
+\[
+\{(x,y):x\in\ga,\ y\in f(x),\ \rv(y)=t_{\ga j}\}.
+\]
+It is $(\code\ga,t_{\ga j})$-definable, the branches reconstruct $f\rest\ga$, and $P(d)$ gives the asserted images.
 \end{proof}
 
